# [Scenario] What would you do if Change Detection is running too often?

## 1. What is it?

"Change detection running too often" means Angular is triggering full (or unnecessarily wide) change-detection passes far more frequently than the UI actually needs to update - a ticking clock, a `mousemove` handler, a WebSocket firing constantly, or a busy `[(ngModel)]` field - and every one of those triggers walks more of the component tree than it needs to, checking bindings on components whose data never actually changed. Nothing is broken - the app still works - but it's burning CPU on checks that produce zero visible difference, which shows up as jank, battery drain, and sluggishness, especially on weaker devices.

## 2. Why is this a problem?

By default, Angular's zone.js patches common async browser APIs (`setTimeout`, `setInterval`, DOM event listeners, `fetch`, WebSocket callbacks, and so on). Any of those firing schedules a change-detection cycle that, by default, walks the **entire** component tree from the root down - not just the part that logically changed. A single high-frequency source (a 300ms timer, a mouse move firing dozens of times a second) can quietly force hundreds of unrelated component checks per second, and because nothing throws an error or looks visibly wrong in a quick glance at the UI, this kind of problem tends to go unnoticed until a profiler - or a real user on a slower device - surfaces it.

## 3. How does it work?

**Diagnosing it first:**
- **Angular DevTools' Profiler tab** is the primary tool - it records a timeline of actual change-detection cycles, showing exactly which components got checked, how long each check took, and how often cycles are firing. A suspiciously regular, high-frequency pattern of full-tree cycles with no corresponding visible UI change is the signature to look for.
- **An ad hoc "check counter"** - a getter that increments every time it's read, dropped into a component's template - is a quick, no-tooling way to see exactly how often a given component's bindings get evaluated (used throughout this note set's demos, including the one below).
- **General browser profiling** (Chrome DevTools' Performance tab) can also surface this indirectly - frequent scripting/style-recalculation spikes tied to zone.js's microtask handling are a hint, even without Angular-specific tooling.

**Fixing it, in roughly the order you'd reach for these:**
1. **Move the actual noisy trigger outside Angular's zone** with `NgZone.runOutsideAngular(() => { ... })`. Code run inside that callback - including a `setInterval`/`setTimeout`/event listener registered there - no longer schedules an app-wide change-detection cycle at all when it fires. You then only re-enter the zone (`ngZone.run(...)`), or run a **local**, targeted `ChangeDetectorRef.detectChanges()` on just the component that actually needs to reflect the new value.
2. **Switch subtrees to `OnPush`**, combined with immutable/stable input references. An `OnPush` component only gets re-checked if one of a few specific things happens (its own input reference changed, a DOM event fired inside its own template, it was manually marked dirty, or an `async` pipe emitted) - so a zone-triggered cycle elsewhere in the app skips it entirely if none of those are true.
3. **Move frequently-changing state to signals.** A signal only re-renders the specific template bindings that actually read it, rather than relying on a global "something happened, check everything" tree walk.
4. **Use `trackBy` (or `track` in the newer `@for` syntax)** on any list, so that even when a check does legitimately happen, Angular doesn't unnecessarily destroy and recreate DOM nodes it could have reused.
5. **Detach a rarely-changing subtree's change detector entirely** with `ChangeDetectorRef.detach()`, and call `.detectChanges()` (or `.reattach()`) manually only when that part of the tree genuinely needs to update - a heavier-handed, more manual version of `OnPush` for parts of the UI that change extremely rarely.
6. **Debounce or throttle the triggering event at its source** - a `mousemove` handler that only updates state every 100ms instead of on every pixel of movement means there's simply less for change detection to react to in the first place, regardless of which of the above techniques you also apply.

